# Layered Architecture (Spring Boot)

## Package Structure

Code is organized by **technical layer**, following Spring Boot conventions.

```
src/main/java/com/duoc/LearningPlatform/
├── controller/          # @RestController — HTTP entry points
│   └── [Entity]Controller.java
├── service/             # @Service — Business logic
│   └── [Entity]Service.java
├── repository/          # Spring Data JPA interfaces
│   └── [Entity]Repository.java
├── model/               # @Entity JPA classes
│   └── [Entity].java
├── dto/                 # Request/Response DTOs
│   ├── [Entity]Request.java
│   └── [Entity]Response.java
└── exception/           # Custom exceptions + global handler
    ├── ResourceNotFoundException.java
    └── GlobalExceptionHandler.java
```

```
src/test/java/com/duoc/LearningPlatform/
├── controller/          # @WebMvcTest — Controller tests
│   └── [Entity]ControllerTest.java
├── service/             # Unit tests — Service logic
│   └── [Entity]ServiceTest.java
├── repository/          # @DataJpaTest — Repository tests
│   └── [Entity]RepositoryTest.java
└── model/               # Unit tests — Validation, behavior
    └── [Entity]Test.java
```

---

## Layers and Responsibilities

### 1. Model (JPA Entities)

- `@Entity` classes representing database tables
- JPA annotations (`@Id`, `@GeneratedValue`, `@Column`, etc.)
- Validation constraints (`@NotNull`, `@Size`, etc.) when appropriate
- Business behavior belongs here when it operates on the entity's own state
- See @.cursor/rules/architecture-model.mdc

### 2. Repository (Data Access)

- Spring Data JPA interfaces extending `JpaRepository`
- Custom query methods using method naming conventions or `@Query`
- **No business logic** — only data access
- See @.cursor/rules/architecture-repository.mdc

### 3. Service (Business Logic)

- `@Service` annotated classes
- All business logic and orchestration lives here
- Receives and returns domain objects or DTOs
- Uses constructor injection for dependencies
- `@Transactional` for operations that modify data
- See @.cursor/rules/architecture-service.mdc

### 4. Controller (HTTP Layer)

- `@RestController` annotated classes
- Handles HTTP requests and responses
- Input validation via `@Valid` and Bean Validation
- Delegates to services — **never contains business logic**
- Maps between DTOs and domain objects
- See @.cursor/rules/architecture-controller.mdc

---

## Dependency Rule

```
Controller → Service → Repository
    ↓           ↓          ↓
  (HTTP)    (Logic)    (Data Access)
              ↓
           Model (used by all layers)
```

- Controllers depend on Services
- Services depend on Repositories
- **All layers** can reference Model classes
- Controllers **never** call Repositories directly
- DTOs are used at the Controller boundary

---

## Spring Dependency Injection

Use **constructor injection** for all dependencies. Spring auto-wires beans by type.

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {

    private final OrderRepository orderRepository;
    private final ProductRepository productRepository;

    public OrderService(
            OrderRepository orderRepository,
            ProductRepository productRepository
    ) {
        this.orderRepository = orderRepository;
        this.productRepository = productRepository;
    }
}
```

---

## Naming Conventions

### Allowed suffixes by layer

| Layer | Suffix | Example |
|-------|--------|---------|
| Model | (entity name) | `Order`, `Product`, `Student` |
| Repository | Repository | `OrderRepository`, `ProductRepository` |
| Service | Service | `OrderService`, `ProductService` |
| Controller | Controller | `OrderController`, `ProductController` |
| DTO (request) | Request | `CreateOrderRequest`, `UpdateProductRequest` |
| DTO (response) | Response | `OrderResponse`, `ProductResponse` |

### File naming

- One file per class/interface
- File name = Class name
- Use PascalCase: `OrderService.java`, `ProductRepository.java`

---

## Non-Negotiable Rules

### ❌ I will NEVER:
1. Put business logic in Controllers
2. Call Repositories directly from Controllers
3. Create "God" classes that do everything (OrderManager, ProductHelper)
4. Use field injection (`@Autowired` on fields) — always constructor injection
5. Skip the Service layer for "simple" CRUD (all access goes through Services)

### ✅ I will ALWAYS:
1. Use constructor injection for dependencies
2. Keep Controllers thin — only HTTP concerns
3. Put business logic in Services
4. Use Spring Data JPA interfaces for Repositories
5. Follow @.cursor/rules/design-naming.mdc and @.cursor/rules/design-functions.mdc for naming and functions
6. Annotate classes with appropriate Spring stereotypes (`@RestController`, `@Service`, `@Repository`)
